        "findings": [
          {
            "source_quote": "τῆς ὑπομονῆς τὸν ἐκ θεοῦ σοι μέγαν ἀποδιδόμενον μισθόν",
            "english_quote": "the great reward from God that will be repaid to you for your endurance",
            "issue": "With μισθόν as its object, ἀποδίδωμι denotes paying or rendering the recompense owed. In modern English, a reward is paid or given, not ordinarily repaid. The draft's collocation can suggest returning a reward previously received rather than paying the reward for endurance.",
            "suggested_revision": "Use “the great reward from God that will be paid to you for your endurance.” Update the lexical rationale to distinguish payment of recompense from repayment of something previously received. This proposed wording requires fresh review.",
            "severity": "minor"
          },
          {
            "source_quote": "μισθοὺς μεγάλους· μετὰ νίκην ἀποδιδόναι πεφυκότι",
            "english_quote": "whose nature it is to repay great rewards after victory",
            "issue": "The same payment-direction problem recurs: μισθοὺς μεγάλους are the rewards the king pays out, not rewards he returns. Preserving the recurrence of ἀποδίδωμι does not require the misleading English collocation “repay great rewards.” The printed high point is an editorial feature, not evidence of textual corruption.",
            "suggested_revision": "Use “whose nature it is to pay great rewards after victory,” keeping the payment wording consistent with the earlier occurrence. This proposed wording requires fresh review; no correction to the supplied Greek is needed.",
            "severity": "minor"
          }
